[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding PHP Superglobals"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP superglobals are built-in variables that are always available in all scopes. This means you can access them from any function, class, or file without having to do anything special. They are associative arrays that contain information about the server, environment, and user."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"$_GET: Accessing URL Parameters"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_GET superglobal is used to collect form data after submitting an HTML form with method="get". It can also collect data sent in the URL. Data in $_GET is visible to everyone in the URL."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing a 'name' parameter from the URL
// URL: example.com?name=John

$name = $_GET['name'];
echo 'Hello, ' . htmlspecialchars($name) . '!'; // Output: Hello, John!"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"$_POST: Handling Form Submissions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_POST superglobal is used to collect form data after submitting an HTML form with method="post". Data in $_POST is not displayed in the URL and is generally used for sensitive data or large amounts of data."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing a 'username' from a form
// <form method="post" action="">
//   <input type="text" name="username">
//   <button type="submit">Submit</button>
// </form>

$username = $_POST['username'];
echo 'Username: ' . htmlspecialchars($username); "},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"$_SESSION: Managing User Sessions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_SESSION superglobal is used to store information about a user session. Session variables are stored on the server and are associated with a unique session ID. To use sessions, you must start a session using session_start()."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Starting a session
session_start();

// Setting a session variable
$_SESSION['username'] = 'JohnDoe';

// Accessing a session variable
echo 'Welcome, ' . htmlspecialchars($_SESSION['username']) . '!';

// Destroying a session
session_destroy();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"$_COOKIE: Storing Data in Cookies"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_COOKIE superglobal is used to access cookies that have been set by the server. Cookies are small text files that are stored on the user's computer. They are often used to remember user preferences or track user activity."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Setting a cookie
setcookie('username', 'JohnDoe', time() + (86400 * 30), '/'); // Expires in 30 days

// Accessing a cookie
echo 'Username: ' . htmlspecialchars($_COOKIE['username']);"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"$_FILES: Handling File Uploads"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_FILES superglobal is used to handle file uploads. It is a multidimensional array containing information about the uploaded file, such as its name, type, size, and temporary location."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing file information
// <form method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data" action="">
//   <input type="file" name="uploaded_file">
//   <button type="submit">Upload</button>
// </form>

$file_name = $_FILES['uploaded_file']['name'];
$file_tmp = $_FILES['uploaded_file']['tmp_name'];

echo 'File Name: ' . htmlspecialchars($file_name);

// Move the uploaded file to a permanent location (ensure proper security checks)
// move_uploaded_file($file_tmp, 'uploads/' . $file_name);"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"$_SERVER: Server and Execution Environment Information"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_SERVER superglobal is an array containing information about headers, paths, and script locations. The entries in this array are created by the web server. It provides access to server-related information, such as the server name, IP address, and request method."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing the server name
echo 'Server Name: ' . htmlspecialchars($_SERVER['SERVER_NAME']);

// Example: Accessing the request method
echo 'Request Method: ' . htmlspecialchars($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD']);"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"$_ENV: Environment Variables"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_ENV superglobal is an array containing environment variables. These variables are set by the operating system or the web server. Note that $_ENV might not be populated by default depending on the server configuration. You might need to configure your server to pass environment variables to PHP."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing an environment variable (if set)
if (isset($_ENV['MY_VARIABLE'])) {
  echo 'My Variable: ' . htmlspecialchars($_ENV['MY_VARIABLE']);
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"$_REQUEST: Combined GET, POST, and COOKIE Data"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The $_REQUEST superglobal is an associative array that, by default, contains the contents of $_GET, $_POST, and $_COOKIE. The order in which these variables are merged is defined by the variables_order directive in php.ini. Using $_REQUEST can be convenient, but it's generally recommended to use $_GET, $_POST, or $_COOKIE directly for better security and clarity."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Accessing a variable that could be in GET or POST
$value = $_REQUEST['my_variable'];
echo 'Value: ' . htmlspecialchars($value);"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Important: Be cautious when using $_REQUEST, as it can make your code vulnerable to security exploits if not handled carefully. Always sanitize and validate user input regardless of which superglobal it comes from."}]
